      <h2 id="nw-bpfman-operator-deploy_bpfman-operator-deploy" class="group scroll-mt-20">Deploying a containerized eBPF program<a href="#nw-bpfman-operator-deploy_bpfman-operator-deploy" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">To run custom networking or security logic on your cluster nodes, you can deploy containerized eBPF programs. You can use containerized eBPF programs to monitor kernel events and manage network traffic efficiently at the node level.</p>
      <p>As a cluster administrator, you can deploy an eBPF program to nodes on your cluster. In this procedure, a sample containerized eBPF program is installed in the <code>go-xdp-counter</code> namespace.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have installed the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>You have an account with administrator privileges.</li>
      <li>You have installed the eBPF Manager Operator.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      To download the manifest, enter the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ curl -L https://github.com/bpfman/bpfman/releases/download/v0.5.1/go-xdp-counter-install-selinux.yaml -o go-xdp-counter-install-selinux.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To deploy the sample eBPF application, enter the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f go-xdp-counter-install-selinux.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"namespace/go-xdp-counter created\nserviceaccount/bpfman-app-go-xdp-counter created\nclusterrolebinding.rbac.authorization.k8s.io/xdp-binding created\ndaemonset.apps/go-xdp-counter-ds created\nxdpprogram.bpfman.io/go-xdp-counter-example created\nselinuxprofile.security-profiles-operator.x-k8s.io/bpfman-secure created"} lang="text" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To confirm that the eBPF sample application deployed successfully, enter the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get all -o wide -n go-xdp-counter"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE   IP             NODE                                 NOMINATED NODE   READINESS GATES\npod/go-xdp-counter-ds-4m9cw   1/1     Running   0          44s   10.129.0.92    ci-ln-dcbq7d2-72292-ztrkp-master-1   <none>           <none>\npod/go-xdp-counter-ds-7hzww   1/1     Running   0          44s   10.130.0.86    ci-ln-dcbq7d2-72292-ztrkp-master-2   <none>           <none>\npod/go-xdp-counter-ds-qm9zx   1/1     Running   0          44s   10.128.0.101   ci-ln-dcbq7d2-72292-ztrkp-master-0   <none>           <none>\n\nNAME                               DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   NODE SELECTOR   AGE   CONTAINERS       IMAGES                                           SELECTOR\ndaemonset.apps/go-xdp-counter-ds   3         3         3       3            3           <none>          44s   go-xdp-counter   quay.io/bpfman-userspace/go-xdp-counter:v0.5.0   name=go-xdp-counter"} lang="text" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To confirm that the example XDP program is running, enter the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get xdpprogram go-xdp-counter-example"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"\nNAME                     BPFFUNCTIONNAME   NODESELECTOR   STATUS\ngo-xdp-counter-example   xdp_stats         {}             ReconcileSuccess"} lang="text" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To confirm that the XDP program is collecting data, enter the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs <pod_name> -n go-xdp-counter"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;pod_name&gt;</code> with the name of an XDP program pod, such as <code>go-xdp-counter-ds-4m9cw</code>.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"2024/08/13 15:20:06 15016 packets received\n2024/08/13 15:20:06 93581579 bytes received\n..."} lang="text" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
